node 5_20260822-020553 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this GEMM kernel, I can see it already implements: CTA-level tiling (BM/BN/BK), double-buffered software pipelining, and thread-level register tiling with vectorized float4 loads. 

The missing structural optimization is **warp-level tiling**. Currently, `threadRow`/`threadCol` are derived from a flat `threadIdx.x` division, meaning a warp (32 consecutive threads) spans across many distinct `threadCol` values when reading `Bs`, and many distinct `threadRow` values when reading `As` — this causes shared-memory bank conflicts since 16 distinct column offsets get accessed per warp. By assigning each warp a contiguous `WM x WN` sub-tile of the block's output, lanes within a warp collapse to far fewer distinct shared-memory addresses per instruction (enabling broadcast reads), reducing bank conflicts and improving load efficiency.

