node 7_20260822-064947 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this code, it's a **reduction kernel** (min reduction along a dimension) — falls under category **(D) Other**, since it's neither GEMM, pure elementwise, nor convolution. 

Key observation: `inner_size = 4095` (from the test shapes: batch=128, dim1=4096 reduced, dim2=4095 inner). The vectorization path requires `inner_size % 4 == 0`, which fails for 4095, so the kernel **falls entirely back to the scalar generic kernel** for all 4095 columns — even though 4092 of those columns (99.93%) could be processed via `float4` vectorized loads, with only the last 3 columns needing scalar handling.

The fix: relax the vectorization condition to only require `inner_size >= 4`. The existing code already correctly handles the remainder via the tail (`min_reduce_kernel_generic`) call with `inner_offset`/`inner_count`, so this only needs a one-line condition change to unlock vectorized loads for the vast majority of the reduction work, cutting global memory transactions ~4x for almost all data.

