node 9_20260822-065908 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this reduction kernel, the main structural inefficiency in the `generic` kernel (which handles all data here since `inner_size=4095` is odd and can never use the `vec4` path due to alignment constraints on the strided reduction dimension) is the use of expensive 64-bit integer division and modulo (`idx / inner_count`, `idx % inner_count`) inside the kernel to decompose the flattened index into `outer_idx`/`local_inner` for every single thread. This is a well-known GPU performance pitfall — integer division/modulo on non-power-of-2 runtime values costs dozens of cycles per thread.

I replace this with a 2D grid launch (`blockIdx.y` = outer index, `blockIdx.x`/`threadIdx.x` grid-stride over inner index), eliminating the division/modulo entirely while preserving identical semantics and memory access patterns (still fully coalesced along the inner dimension). This same restructuring is applied consistently to the `vec4` kernel as well.

