Currently `TILE_D=4` with `K=3, stride=1, dilation=1` gives a halo overhead of `(SD - TILE_D)/TILE_D = 2/4 = 50%` — meaning half of the loaded shared-memory input tile along the depth dimension is "wasted" halo relative to useful output tile size. Since the depth dimension `D=64` is large, increasing `TILE_D` to 8 reduces this halo overhead to `2/8 = 25%`, improving input-tile reuse per shared-memory load (fewer redundant halo loads relative to useful compute), reduces the number of thread blocks along depth (halving `num_tiles_d`), and better amortizes the weight staging cost — all while keeping shared memory usage well within limits (~24KB, safely under the 48KB default) and block size at a reasonable 256 threads.

