These staging loops (`for (int i = threadIdx.x; i < weight_slice_size; i += blockDim.x)` and similarly for input) require many sequential iterations per thread since `blockDim.x` is so small (weight_slice_size=3360, iw_tile*channels~4320), severely limiting memory-level parallelism and occupancy during the memory-bound staging phases, which dominate given the large kernel volume (kD*kH*kW=105) and channel count.

By reducing `OW_COARSEN` from 4 to 2, we double `threads_per_block` to 64, doubling the parallelism available for the cooperative shared-memory loads (weight staging and input tile staging), improving memory coalescing/throughput during those phases, while still retaining register-blocked reuse of shared-memory weight values across 2 output elements per thread during the compute phase.

