node 7_20260822-105031 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this kernel, it's a **reduction/elementwise-style kernel** computing per-row squared distances for triplet margin loss. It already implements vectorized float4 loads, grid-stride looping over rows, and warp-shuffle-based reduction. 

One structural gap: the kernel uses `#pragma unroll 4` on the main float4 loop combined with several live float4/float temporaries (`av, pv, nv, dax..daw, dnx..dnw, sum_pos, sum_neg`), which drives up register usage per thread. With `BLOCK_SIZE=512` threads/block, high register pressure can limit the number of resident blocks per SM (occupancy), even though the grid is large. Adding `__launch_bounds__` gives the compiler an explicit occupancy target, capping register allocation and allowing more blocks to be scheduled concurrently per SM without changing any semantics or precision.

