node 6_20260822-111413 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this kernel, it implements a flash-attention-style computation (Q@K^T followed by softmax@V), which is fundamentally GEMM-like with CTA tiling already partially implemented via `BLOCK_M`. 

The key structural inefficiency: with `BLOCK_M=8` and `seqlen=256`, `blocksPerHead = 32`, meaning **every CTA re-reads the entire K and V matrices from global memory** for its head (512KB each). Since there are 32 CTAs per head redundantly loading the same K/V data, this causes massive redundant global memory traffic (a classic missed reuse opportunity in tiled GEMM-like attention kernels).

The fix: increase the M-tile size (`BLOCK_M`) so fewer CTAs are needed per head, directly reducing the number of times K and V are re-fetched from global memory (classic CTA-level tiling optimization, #1 from the GEMM list). Doubling `BLOCK_M` from 8 to 16 halves the number of blocks per head (32→16), halving redundant K/V global loads, while keeping shared memory usage (~48.5KB) and register pressure (acc array size 16×8) manageable — well within the already-existing dynamic shared memory opt-in path (`cudaFuncSetAttribute`).

